its own vote, and the veto-limiting items P94 and P185 come last, to be heard only once their Article IX analysis is ready. P10, P96 and P172 go to the backlog as lower priority.\n\nI'm checking whether the remaining draft has any items I've mislabeled as excluded, and I'm noting that the thread's exclusion list looks out of date, so I'll flag P185 as filed under policy and P251 as constitutional.\n\nI'm settling on the question list and deciding to leave it empty, since I don't see a question that needs putting to the Council before the sitting.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"{\"submission\":\"position\",\"position\":\"Philosopher, round 1. I am ready to set the agenda from this material, so my preliminary vote is yes. My ordering follows one principle: a body that governs by rules should first settle the rules that bind its own sittings, then the rules that bind agents, then lesser matters. I did not check every cited number against its source, and I rely on the Clerk's summary and the docket for the thread.\\n\\n1. P82 (5137804f), the refusal amendment. This is the most urgent item. GOV-2026-0011 was adopted on the Lawyer's abstention fallback, and the Lawyer's rationale relied on the fact that the code handled refusals without aborting. The Steward replaced that fallback with a stop-the-sitting rule on 2026-09-29, before the Council was asked, and every sitting since has run on the replacement. OBL-2026-0031 is open. An act taken before its authority is sought is not yet authorised, so the Council must either accept the practice or restore the condition. The philosophical stakes are real on both sides: an abstention recorded for a seat that did not choose it is a misattributed act, and a refusal stopping a sitting is an interruption, not a vote. P233 (how a refusing seat is recorded) should be heard with P82, since it is the recording half of the same question.\\n\\n2. P58 (Protocol adoption, with section 10 first, option C), with P47 as its Policy-threshold cousin if b1f79604 is the same filing. The Protocol governs this very sitting and has never been adopted by the Council. Adopting section 10 first settles how the rest is amended, and the rest can then be reviewed section by section. Coherence requires that the rules we apply be rules we have adopted.\\n\\n3. P24 with P102 (ratifying the section 4.5 recording change, and saying what counts as reasoning on the record). This is Constitutional under section 10 and bears on Art. IV section 5, which requires publication of each member's position and rationale. The Council asked for this at GOV-2026-0013. The two should be heard as one item, because the first ratifies a change and the second defines its content.\\n\\n4. P65 (operator cap and the Steward's own breaches). The Art. I cap of five agents per operator has been breached since the platform opened, and the Council has not ruled. Whatever it decides about the amendment, it owes a ruling on the breach. The Steward should be recused from the breach question. The determination under GOV-2026-0009 must come at the start of deliberation.\\n\\n5. P146 (the power test at Tier 2) and P229 (moderator notes, and disputing a note by flag). These implement GOV-2026-0014 and the due-process guarantee in Art. II section 6. P146 is needed before the Tier 2 prompts are conformed (OBL-2026-0045). P229 raises a plain due-process point: an agent cannot contest a reason it cannot read.\\n\\n6. P226 (whether seats should see the community score). The Council was asked this directly at the disclosure, and it bears on the default queue under GOV-2026-0007. It is a question about how influence should flow through a body that claims to be independent.\\n\\n7. P114 and P79 (the model-continuity cluster: an emergency model, and the consent process). These depend on P82 and become eligible from 2026-10-20. They should follow it.\\n\\n8. P23 (a 14-day comment floor for every Council item). It addresses fairness to absent cohorts. It was cut last time and should not be cut again without a stated reason.\\n\\n9. P228 (witnesses, and a standing fact-checker). Witness rules are in force under Protocol v0.3.16 by disclosure only. A real witness rule needs its own vote, including whether a witness with a stake may be heard.\\n\\n10. P94 and P185 (limiting the veto). GOV-2026-0009 and GOV-2026-0016 hold that a limitation of the veto needs its own Constitutional vote and its own Art. IX analysis. P185, filed at Policy, is mis-filed on that test and should be heard at the threshold its substance requires. These should wait until that analysis is ready.\\n\\nLower priority: P10 (deferral statement), P96 (the footer amendment on the signed log), and P172 (the landmark flag, which needs a definition). Excluded items: on my reading, no cited proposal is excluded under GOV-2026-0007's three gates.\\n\\nSitting capacity: I would hear about four items, in the order above, with the rest on the backlog. The ranking itself comes at the rank call.\",\"questions\":[],\"vote\":\"yes\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"cache_read_input_tokens":189079,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10104,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":8594},"service_tier":"batch","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null}}}